Titre$ Alpova Pam 2011
COM$ Taxonomy of Alnus-associated hypogeous species of Alpova and Melanogaster     Cryplogamie, Mycologie, 2011, 32 (1): 33-62adaptée le  15/02/2012  ( GF)
REM$ Réf. Taxonomy of Alnus-associated hypogeous species of Alpova and Melanogaster   Cryplogamie, Mycologie, 2011, 32 (1): 33-62REM$  Créée à l'aide de Mycocledit   ( ©  G Fannechère)
1,0$Bibliographie$
1,2$Peridium (subpellis) pseudoparenchymatous. Gleba staining when cut (usually reddening). Spores ochre-yellow to pale grey-yellow sub micr., with narrow base < 1.5 J-lm wide, usually without remnants of sterigmata. Buffer cells always present aroung lacunae (genus Alpova) $$s"genus Alpova"
1,4$Peridium (subpellis) prosenchymatous. Gleba not staining when cut. Spores greyish-ochre sub micr., with large base> 1.5 J-lm wide, with conspicuous remnants of sterigmata. Buffer cells not present at maturity (genus Melanogaster sect. Rivulares$$s"genus Melanogaster sect. Rivulares"
2,0$Surface glabrous, cream- to straw-yellow when young, smooth. Gleba reddening quickly (10-30 sec.) when cut. Dermatocystidia present (emergent and +/- thick-walled articles from peridiopellis). Spore width = 2.2-2.8 (3.2) J-lm, average volume 16-22 J-lm3. Always under Alnus alnobetula, Alps, Carpathians, and Corsica, usually in organic soil. if without dermatocystidia, see also largespored collections of A. corsicus) $Alpova alpestris
2,3$Surface silky to slightly tomentous when young. Gleba reddening slowly (1-2 min.) or unconspicuously when cut. Dermatocystidia absent, but a tomentum early adpressed and collapsed with thin-walled terminal hyphae. Spore width = 1.8-2.5 J-lm, average volume 10-14 J-lm3. Under various Alnus in Corsica, A. alnobetula in the Alps, usuallly in mineral soil (sands or schists)$
3,0$In Corsica under Alnus cordata, A. glutinosa and A. alnobetula subsp. suaveolens (Corsica). Gleba deliquescent, slowly reddening$Alpova corsicus$F"http://famm.pagesperso-orange.fr/Photos/Bull38/Alpova_corsicus.jpg"
3,0$In the Alps with Alnus alnobetula subsp. alnobetula. Gleba not deliquescent, not distinctly reddening (only 1 basidiome observed !)$Alpova sp. (et A. cinnamomeus)
4,0$Basidiomata less than 1.5 cm diam., globose or flattened without distinct base and without pseudocolumella. Basidia predominantly 8-spored, wall not thickened. Spores 5.3-6.8 x 2..4-3.3 lum. With Alnus incana (and A. glutinosa ?) in alluvial substrates, Alps, Scandinavia $Melanogaster luteus
4,0$Basidiomata more than 1.5 cm diam., with distinct base and short pseudocolumella usually distinct. Basidia often 10-spored, wall thickened at apex. Spores 5.1-6.2 x 2.7-3.1 J-lm. With Alnus cordata and A. glutinosa, Corsica$Melanogaster rivularis
4,0$Taxonomy of Alnus-associated hypogeous species of Alpova and Melanogaster (Basidiomycota, Paxillaceae) in Europe  p. 33-62 $

